From 8225880a9f60e1d62576bcfd887cd83310d33709 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: AkiraZheng <1428384878@qq.com>
Date: Sat, 19 Sep 2026 09:06:51 +0800
Subject: [PATCH 3/4] KVM: arm64: Document the benign races in
 stage2_wrprotect_walker()

The dedicated write-protect walker updates stage-2 entries with a
plain WRITE_ONCE, concurrent with two hardware updates; neither can
lose information, but nothing in the code said so.

The access-flag race was already documented in the attr walker this
one was derived from and the comment was lost in the rewrite: at
worst an AF update is lost and the next access faults it in again.

The dirty-state race never touches the same entry state: hardware
only promotes a writable-clean entry (S2AP[1] clear) while this
walker only writes entries whose old value had S2AP[1] set. The one
window is the DBM removal on writable-clean blocks, where a promotion
landing between our read and our write is discarded by the demotion
back to read-only. That is safe twice over: the promotion recorded an
HDBSS buffer entry, which preserves the dirty information for the
migration bitmap, and the folio account is already covered by the
speculative fault-in dirtying that accompanies every write-permitting
installation. The next write faults (DBM now clear) and takes the
software path.

Also state why harvesting the block base PA is sufficient: block
mappings are backed by a single hugetlbfs/THP folio.

No functional change.

Co-Authored-By: Claude <noreply@anthropic.com>
Signed-off-by: Tian Zheng <zhengtian10@huawei.com>
---
 arch/arm64/kvm/hyp/pgtable.c | 37 +++++++++++++++++++++++++++++++-----
 1 file changed, 32 insertions(+), 5 deletions(-)

diff --git a/arch/arm64/kvm/hyp/pgtable.c b/arch/arm64/kvm/hyp/pgtable.c
index 08cfce90396c..9d34f9b596b2 100644
--- a/arch/arm64/kvm/hyp/pgtable.c
+++ b/arch/arm64/kvm/hyp/pgtable.c
@@ -1318,17 +1318,44 @@ static int stage2_wrprotect_walker(const struct kvm_pgtable_visit_ctx *ctx,
 		new &= ~KVM_PTE_LEAF_ATTR_HI_S2_DBM;
 
 	/*
-	 * A writable-dirty page is about to lose its dirty state. If
-	 * hardware promoted it (HAFDBS/HDBSS), the pending buffer entry
-	 * has not been drained yet and the CLEAR ioctl can race with an
-	 * unmap that follows; harvest the folio account now so the dirty
-	 * information survives the transition regardless.
+	 * A writable-dirty entry is about to lose its dirty state.  If
+	 * hardware promoted it (HAFDBS/HDBSS), the pending HDBSS buffer
+	 * entry has not been drained yet and this write-protect can race
+	 * with an unmap that follows; harvest the folio account now so
+	 * the dirty information survives the transition regardless.
+	 *
+	 * A block mapping is backed by a single (hugetlbfs/THP) folio, so
+	 * dirtying the folio containing the block base PA covers the
+	 * whole mapping.
 	 */
 	if (kvm_pte_valid(ctx->old) && ctx->old != new &&
 	    (ctx->old & KVM_PTE_LEAF_ATTR_LO_S2_S2AP_W) &&
 	    ctx->mm_ops->mark_page_dirty)
 		ctx->mm_ops->mark_page_dirty(kvm_pte_to_phys(ctx->old));
 
+	/*
+	 * The plain WRITE_ONCE races with two hardware updates while the
+	 * entry is being written; both are benign, but for different
+	 * reasons:
+	 *
+	 * - The access flag.  We might race with the CPU trying to set
+	 *   AF, worst-case the AF update is lost and will be set on the
+	 *   next access instead.
+	 *
+	 * - The dirty state.  Hardware only promotes a writable-clean
+	 *   entry (S2AP[1] clear) to writable-dirty; this walker only
+	 *   writes entries whose old value had S2AP[1] set, so the two
+	 *   never touch the same entry state.  The one window is the DBM
+	 *   removal on blocks, which produces a non-identity write for a
+	 *   writable-clean block: if hardware promotes it after our read
+	 *   but before our write, our write demotes the entry back to
+	 *   read-only and discards the promotion.  That is safe: the
+	 *   promotion already recorded an HDBSS buffer entry, so the
+	 *   dirty information survives, and the folio account is covered
+	 *   by the speculative fault-in dirtying that accompanies every
+	 *   write-permitting installation.  The next write to the entry
+	 *   faults (DBM now clear) and takes the software path.
+	 */
 	if (kvm_pte_valid(ctx->old) && ctx->old != new)
 		WRITE_ONCE(*ctx->ptep, new);
 
